WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Speaker 1: All right. I have some amazing video&nbsp;
for you today. Now, if you're allergic to cringe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.640
I would tune away, but if you relish it, if you&nbsp;
live for the cringe, then this is the segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.040
for you. I've been talking now for a very long&nbsp;
time about how the Republican Party has moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.280
away from even pretending to be about policy. This&nbsp;
sort of started during the civil rights movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:27.280
in the late 50s, 60s and into the 70s.&nbsp;
You can actually trace Republicans not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.120
being about policy all the way back&nbsp;
to that. But in the more modern era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.000
the Republican Party abandoning policy was&nbsp;
accelerated when Newt Gingrich took over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.360
the control of speakership of the House of&nbsp;
Representatives in 1994, when kind of dimwitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.040
George W. Bush became president. Republicans moved&nbsp;
further away from being about policy and instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:53.440
being about cultural issues, et cetera, and memes&nbsp;
and slogans. When Barack Obama became president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.880
his being half-black triggered so many of the&nbsp;
more racist and xenophobic right wingers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.400
the focus further went away from policy and more&nbsp;
into personality stuff and culture war issues. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.080
under Donald Trump, they pretended to even care&nbsp;
about policy. And Joe Biden pointed this out at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.040
